*.sh text eol=lf

# Changelog fragments are one file per branch (see scripts/changelogFragments.js),
# so two branches normally touch two different paths and never conflict. `union`
# covers the residual case: two branches whose names share a last segment derive
# the same slug and write the same fragment, where keeping both bullets is the
# right answer. A fragment is short-lived — created on a branch, deleted at
# collection — so it has no cross-release failure mode.
#
# Deliberately NOT applied to .changelog/NEXT.md. It looks like the same
# append-only shape, but `/do:release` renames NEXT.md to v{version}.md and
# starts a fresh one, and git does not pair that rename (the path still exists
# on main). A branch that outlives a release is then a plain modify/modify, and
# union resolves it by keeping BOTH sides — silently reviving the entire
# previous release's entries into the next release's notes, with no conflict and
# no warning. A loud conflict there is the correct outcome.
.changelog/next/*.md merge=union
